course: dictionary
lesson: 9536
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. «Stocking» can mean something you wear, something you hang up at Christmas, or a job done in a shop.
explain: «In many French families the big Christmas meal is on the evening of 24 December, and children traditionally leave their shoes by the tree or the fireplace for le Père Noël, not stockings.» The clues are Christmas and le Père Noël. Here stockings are the big socks hung up for presents.
explain: «Nylon was invented in the 1930s to replace silk; it was cheaper and stronger, so people queued for hours for the first nylon stockings.» Queued for hours and nylon show that stockings are the long, thin things worn on the legs. Always check: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A stocking is a long, thin covering for the foot and leg, usually worn by women, often in pairs.
fact: A Christmas stocking is a big sock hung up on Christmas Eve to be filled with small presents.
fact: «Stocking» is also the -ing form of «stock»: stocking the shelves means putting goods out in a shop, and stocking a pond means filling it with fish.
fact: A «stocking filler» (UK) or «stocking stuffer» (US) is a small, cheap present.
exercise: «...people queued for hours for the first nylon stockings.» What are stockings in this sentence? | Shelves in a shop | Fish in a pond | *Long, thin coverings for the legs | Christmas presents
exercise: Mum says she is stocking the shelves. What does stocking mean here? | Wearing thin socks | *Putting goods out for sale | Going to bed early | Hanging up a sock
exercise: In France children leave shoes for le Père Noël, not stockings. What is a Christmas stocking? | *A big sock hung up to be filled with presents | A small, cheap present for a shop | A kind of shoe worn in the rain | A tall boot for snow
test: A small, cheap present is called a stocking filler in the UK. What is it called in the US? | Christmas stocking | Stocking rate | *Stocking stuffer | Stocking the shelves
test: Farmers talk about a stocking rate. What does it measure? | How many nylon stockings are made each day | *How many animals there are per area of land | How many shoes a child owns | How many presents fit in a stocking
exam: What does «stocking» mean in «stocking a pond with fish»? | A sock for Christmas Eve | *To fill something with supplies | A small present | To wear on the leg
